{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"bc3c63c6d30de91a55b18cd51f0a1767869f8f38d193dcc09463b31b37f639b7","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_209de7eaf5c05b499efe58c603aa1f8b74a3b2bd3334cf69b191a8e5cb3aeccd","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a technically sound and well-motivated study on customized numerical precision for DNN inference. The methodology is rigorous, combining software emulation with hardware synthesis to validate accuracy-efficiency trade-offs. The proposed search technique is a significant contribution that reduces the computational cost of precision selection. The work is complete, with clear experimental validation across multiple network architectures and a thorough analysis of error sources.","paper_id":"BJ_MGwqlg","paper_identity_sha256":"f09c338a695732fef571f72cc4cf9692a613334da97d39783df0392c12bd887b","prompt_identity_sha256":"3f9c29cda874297475c9e12a9849119e3c5fd58e513d9a47cc24eda33451b721","raw_response_sha256":"fc69183ee05059adc0d799b64f08c6c6b0d055945adee90846e29ac16e66308e","request_identity_sha256":"29b3fce0dc3e4eb0e082fa5b1022528909b8c8572690b39ae6bd650dfba95032","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"fedd5f25e43b1e170b327078f258b0f03cc166f93658a042ca5c4888929cbd68","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-010","SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-019"],"review_text":"The proposed method for customized precision is well-formulated. The paper clearly defines the mathematical representations for both fixed-point and floating-point formats, including the specific bit allocations for mantissa, exponent, and bias. The hardware implications are logically derived from these representations, correctly identifying that reduced bit-widths lead to lower delay and area, which in turn allows for higher parallelism and clock frequencies. The search technique, which uses the coefficient of determination of last-layer activations to predict accuracy, is a sound heuristic that avoids the need for exhaustive full-network evaluation. The conceptual link between internal activation error and final classification accuracy is well-supported by the experimental results.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-015","SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-021","SRC-022","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The experimental design is comprehensive and appropriate for the claims made. The authors evaluate a diverse set of neural networks, ranging from small-scale models (LeNet-5, CIFARNET) to production-grade architectures (AlexNet, VGG, GoogLeNet), which allows for a robust analysis of how network size and structure impact precision requirements. The use of standard benchmark validation sets (ImageNet, CIFAR-10, MNIST) ensures that the accuracy metrics are comparable to prior work. The efficiency evaluation is grounded in actual hardware synthesis using industry-standard tools (Synopsys Design Compiler and PrimeTime) targeting a 28nm process, which provides a realistic assessment of speedup and energy savings. The ablation of the search method, comparing exhaustive search against the proposed model with varying numbers of refinement samples, effectively validates the utility of the technique.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-015","SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-021"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient detail to reproduce the core methodology. The authors specify the use of the Caffe framework for software emulation and describe the modification to truncate mantissa and exponent bits after each arithmetic operation. The hardware evaluation details, including the specific EDA tools (Synopsys Design Compiler and PrimeTime) and the target manufacturing process (28nm), are clearly stated. The dataset splits are defined as the canonical benchmark validation sets for each network. While the paper does not explicitly mention the release of code, the level of detail provided regarding the precision formats, hardware design flow, and search algorithm parameters (such as the use of last-layer activations and the linear correlation model) is adequate for a researcher to implement a similar system.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-006","SRC-035"],"review_text":"The paper effectively positions its work relative to prior research by highlighting the limitations of previous studies, which primarily focused on small-scale networks and fixed-point representations. The authors explicitly contrast their findings with works by Gupta et al. (2015), Cavigelli et al. (2015), and others, demonstrating that conclusions drawn from small networks do not generalize to larger, production-grade DNNs. The comparison is fair and grounded in the specific design space explored (customized floating-point vs. fixed-point). The related work section appropriately distinguishes the proposed hardware-centric approach from alternative methods like spiking neural networks or redundancy-based compression, clarifying the scope of the contribution.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-016","SRC-021","SRC-031","SRC-033"],"review_text":"The statistical rigor of the evaluation is appropriate for the domain. The authors use standard accuracy metrics (top-1 and top-5) on full validation sets, which provides a robust measure of inference performance. For the search technique, the authors employ cross-validation to build the accuracy model, ensuring that the model is not overfit to the specific network being searched. The validation of the search method includes a comparison against an exhaustive search baseline, demonstrating that the proposed method achieves the same optimal configurations with a fraction of the computational cost. While the paper does not report confidence intervals or significance tests for the accuracy differences, the use of large validation sets and the deterministic nature of the hardware synthesis results provide sufficient confidence in the reported improvements.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"905e3de0cecbb0b5b5bc7601dcd2ad9f0266429d4dda81c0b17d13173239e089","paper_id":"BJ_MGwqlg","paper_ordinal":32,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"fc69183ee05059adc0d799b64f08c6c6b0d055945adee90846e29ac16e66308e","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BJ_MGwqlg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"7e616f1a7fcc8ba8ab54a01775f864ec6b97ee2fc34e8d330e6a91a3bdb88549","request_identity_sha256":"29b3fce0dc3e4eb0e082fa5b1022528909b8c8572690b39ae6bd650dfba95032","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1230,"prompt_tokens":16715,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":17945},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"29b3fce0dc3e4eb0e082fa5b1022528909b8c8572690b39ae6bd650dfba95032","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}